\f0\b\fs36 Theory & Methodology
\f1\b0\fs28 \
\

\f0\b Geometric Brownian Motion -
\f1\b0 \
\
Stock prices are modelled by the stochastic differential equation:\
\
\
dS = \uc0\u956  \'b7 S \'b7 dt + \u963  \'b7 S \'b7 dW\
\
\
where dW is a Wiener process. Under the risk-neutral measure, the drift \uc0\u956  is replaced by the risk-free rate r. The exact solution (via It\'f4's lemma) gives:\
\
\
S(t+\uc0\u916 t) = S(t) \'b7 exp[(r \u8722  \u963 \'b2/2) \'b7 \u916 t + \u963  \'b7 \u8730 \u916 t \'b7 Z],  Z ~ N(0,1)\
\
\
The \uc0\u8722 \u963 \'b2/2 term is the It\'f4 correction, which adjusts for the convexity of the exponential function applied to a random variable.\
\

\f0\b Monte Carlo Pricing -
\f1\b0 \
\
- Simulate an independent stock price paths to expiry\
- Compute the payoff for each path  - max(S_T \uc0\u8722  K, 0) for calls, max(K \u8722  S_T, 0) for puts\
- Average the payoffs and discount to present value: Price = e^(\uc0\u8722 rT) \'b7 mean(payoffs)\
\
By the Law of Large Numbers, this converges to the true price.\
\

\f0\b Black-Scholes Formula -
\f1\b0 \
\
Used as the derived benchmark:\
\
\
d\uc0\u8321  = [ln(S/K) + (r + \u963 \'b2/2) \'b7 T] / (\u963  \'b7 \u8730 T)\
d\uc0\u8322  = d\u8321  \u8722  \u963  \'b7 \u8730 T\
\
Call = S \'b7 N(d\uc0\u8321 ) \u8722  K \'b7 e^(\u8722 rT) \'b7 N(d\u8322 )\
Put  = K \'b7 e^(\uc0\u8722 rT) \'b7 N(\u8722 d\u8322 ) \u8722  S \'b7 N(\u8722 d\u8321 )\
\
\

\f0\b The Greeks -
\f1\b0 \
\
The Greeks are partial derivatives of the option price with respect to each input parameter. They quantify how sensitive the option is to changes in market conditions and are essential for hedging and risk management.\
\
| Greek | Definition | Formula (Call) | Interpretation |\
\
|  Delta  | \uc0\u8706 V/\u8706 S | N(d\u8321 ) | Price change per $1 stock move. Also approximates probability of expiring ITM. |\
|  Gamma  | \uc0\u8706 \'b2V/\u8706 S\'b2 | n(d\u8321 ) / (S\'b7\u963 \'b7\u8730 T) | Rate of change of Delta. Peaks ATM near expiry. Same for calls and puts. |\
|  Theta  |  \uc0\u8706 V/\u8706 t | \u8722 [S\'b7n(d\u8321 )\'b7\u963  / (2\u8730 T)] \u8722  rKe^(\u8722 rT)N(d\u8322 ) | Daily time decay. Almost always negative \'97 options are wasting assets. |\
|  Vega  |  \uc0\u8706 V/\u8706 \u963  | S \'b7 n(d\u8321 ) \'b7 \u8730 T | Price change per 1% volatility move. Same for calls and puts. |\
|  Rho  |  \uc0\u8706 V/\u8706 r | K\'b7T\'b7e^(\u8722 rT)\'b7N(d\u8322 ) | Price change per 1% rate move. Positive for calls, negative for puts. |\
\
Where N(x) is the standard normal CDF and n(x) is the standard normal PDF.\
\
The calculated Greeks are gotten via the finite difference "bump-and-revalue" method, which works for any pricing model including exotics:\
\
\
\uc0\u8706 V/\u8706 x \u8776  [V(x + h) \u8722  V(x \u8722  h)] / (2h)        (central difference)\
\uc0\u8706 \'b2V/\u8706 x\'b2 \u8776  [V(x+h) \u8722  2V(x) + V(x\u8722 h)] / h\'b2   (second derivative)\
\
\

